          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections, three this year. Is recurrent infection something to escalate, and what does guidance describe about managing it?",
          "answer": "The safety check rated your description as routine. It did not match any emergency pattern, but that is not a clearance. If you develop any of the warning signs below, seek care promptly.\n\n**Call emergency services immediately** if a fever comes with confusion, fast breathing or breathlessness, a fast heart rate, dizziness on standing, very little or no urine for many hours, mottled or very pale skin, or a rash that does not fade when pressed. These can signal sepsis, a life-threatening response to infection. Also seek urgent assessment for fever with pain in the side or back, shaking chills, vomiting that stops you keeping fluids down, confusion, any symptoms during pregnancy or if you have one kidney or a transplant, or symptoms that get worse despite 48 hours of treatment. Blood in the urine is not on the emergency list, but it is a reason to contact a clinician.\n\nThree urine infections in a year counts as recurrent infection. The usual definition is two or more in six months, or three or more in a year (Uncomplicated urinary tract infection guidance, Infectious diseases and urology society). That is a reason to raise it with your doctor or clinician, but it is not an emergency on its own. Guidance says a urine culture is useful in recurrent infection, because it can show which germ is involved and which antibiotics would work.\n\nGuidance lists behaviour changes as a first measure, though the passages I retrieved do not say which changes they mean. After menopause, vaginal oestrogen is described as a preventive option. Where infections are frequent and disabling, antibiotic prevention, taken continuously or after sex, is described as an option, weighed against the risk of antibiotic resistance. Cranberry products and drinking more fluids are described as having limited and mixed evidence.\n\nAuthorities also differ on early treatment. Some European guidance supports delaying or avoiding antibiotics for mild bladder infections as a shared decision with the patient. Other guidance keeps immediate antibiotics as the default. The disagreement is about how much antibiotic use is worth avoiding, not about the underlying facts.\n\nThe passages I retrieved do not grade the evidence behind each measure, so I cannot tell you how strong the support is for any of them. Please ask your clinician how well each option is supported and which one suits you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
